# qwen3.8:27b-bare — the cloud→local failover brain (profiles/
# local-failover-qwen27.env). "bare" is the whole point: NO baked system prompt,
# 32K window, and a model that can still call tools.
#
# Upgraded 2026-08-16 from qwen3.5:27b-bare. Note the version jump skips 3.7:
# Qwen never shipped a 3.7 on Ollama, the library goes 3.5 → 3.6 → 3.8, so
# "3.7" in a request means "the next one up" and resolves here.
#
# THIS UPGRADE REQUIRED AN OLLAMA UPGRADE, which is the part to remember if you
# ever roll back. Ollama 0.23.4 REFUSES this tag: `ollama pull qwen3.8:27b`
# exits 0 having downloaded nothing and prints only "Please download the latest
# version at: https://ollama.com/download". It is not a network failure and it
# does not say "unsupported"; a pull that transfers 0 bytes and succeeds is what
# a too-old client looks like. This host went 0.23.4 → 0.32.13 (Homebrew) on
# 2026-08-16. qwen3.6:27b, by contrast, pulls fine on 0.23.4 — so 3.6 is the
# rollback that does NOT need the daemon downgraded, and 3.5 is the one that
# does not need it either.
#
# THIS DIRECTORY EXISTS TO STAY OUT OF ../build.sh's GLOB, and that is not
# theoretical. build.sh appends prompts/claude-fable-5-system.md as a SYSTEM
# directive to every *.Modelfile it builds and defaults to building ALL of them.
# Measured 2026-08-06: every local qwen3.5 tag (9b, 4b, and each -64k/-128k/
# -256k failover tier) carries 186,647 bytes — roughly 43K tokens — of baked
# system prompt as a result. A subdirectory does not match `*.Modelfile`, so a
# bare `./build.sh` cannot reach this file.
#
# BUILD FROM THE REGISTRY TAG, NEVER FROM A LOCAL ONE. `FROM` inherits the
# base's SYSTEM directive, so `FROM qwen3.5:9b` produces a "bare" model carrying
# all 43K tokens. That happened on the first attempt here and was not subtle:
# the model answered from the baked prompt's tool vocabulary, inventing a
# `weather_fetch` tool that the request never defined. qwen3.8:27b is a pristine
# registry pull (verified: `ollama show --system` returns 0 bytes), so this tag
# inherits nothing.
#
# BUILD FROM THE GGUF TAG, NOT THE int4/MLX ONE — this is a memory-safety
# requirement, not a preference. Ollama serves int4/mlx tags on its MLX engine
# (`ollama runner --mlx-engine ...`), and that engine applies num_ctx from
# NEITHER the Modelfile NOR OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH: it loads the model's native
# window and its runner is spawned with no --ctx-size argument at all. The
# llama.cpp engine (`--ollama-engine`, used for GGUF/Q4_K_M) applies both.
#
# `qwen3.8:27b` (no suffix) IS the GGUF build — verified from the manifest
# before pulling, and that check is the cheap way to stay safe:
#   curl -s -H "Accept: application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.v2+json" \
#     https://registry.ollama.ai/v2/library/qwen3.8/manifests/27b | \
#     jq -r '.layers[].mediaType'
# GGUF is a SINGLE `...image.model` layer; MLX is many small `tensor` layers.
# qwen3.8:27b returns license + model + params + projector = 4 layers, one of
# them `model` → GGUF. The sibling `qwen3.8:27b-mlx` is the one to avoid.
#
# THE `projector` LAYER IS NEW IN 3.8 and 3.5 had no equivalent: this tag is
# multimodal (vision). That is the entire reason it is 17.7GB on disk against
# 3.5's 17.4GB. Bare mode never sends it an image, so the projector is dead
# weight here rather than a feature — budget for it, do not plan around it.
#
# Measured 2026-08-12 on this host (Ollama 0.23.4), and RE-VERIFIED 2026-08-16
# on 0.32.13, same server, same env:
#   phi4-mini:3.8b   GGUF  native 131072  num_ctx unset  → loaded at  32768 ✅
#   qwen3.5:27b-bare MLX   native 262144  num_ctx 32768  → loaded at 262144 ❌
# phi4-mini carries no num_ctx of its own, so its clamp can only have come from
# OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH — which is what proves the env reaches the GGUF path and
# is ignored on the MLX one. Re-running that phi4-mini control immediately after
# the 0.32.13 upgrade still printed 32768, which is how we know the upgrade did
# not quietly change the clamp this whole file depends on. RE-RUN IT after any
# future Ollama upgrade; it is two commands and it is the only cheap proof.
#
# Cost of getting this wrong, observed in production: this tag ran at a 262144
# window on a 36GB host. MLX allocates KV lazily, so the int4 build loaded at
# ~15GB and GREW toward ~32GB as a session filled the window — which is why the
# sizing measurement in this file was honest and the machine still ran out of
# memory. A 15GB floor was recorded as if it were a steady state.
# "Out of application memory" is what you get; the RAM% readout and Force Quit's
# app list both stay innocent, because a 100%-GPU Ollama daemon appears in
# neither.
#
#   ollama create qwen3.8:27b-bare -f modelfiles/bare/qwen3.8-27b-bare.Modelfile
#
# Sizing: 17.7GB on disk. RESIDENT SIZE IS NOT YET MEASURED ON THIS TAG — the
# predecessor qwen3.5:27b-bare measured **23GB** resident at num_ctx 32768 /
# OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL=2, and 3.8 should land near it plus the projector, but
# "should" is exactly the word that preceded both panics on this host. Run this
# and write the real number here before trusting the tier under memory pressure:
#   ollama run qwen3.8:27b-bare "hi" >/dev/null && ollama ps
# `ollama list` reports the on-disk figure and will mislead you by roughly half.
#
# Plan against the measured GGUF number, never a computed one. The int4 build the
# 3.5 tag once used was recorded at ~15GB, which was a real reading of a lazily
# allocated floor rather than a steady state — see the MLX note above. Paying
# more at rest to get a window that actually holds is the right trade: bounded
# beats unbounded.
#
# Budget against the ~27GB Metal wired ceiling, not against 36GB. Wired pages
# cannot be evicted, so over-committing them panics this host instead of raising
# OOM (it did, twice, on 2026-07-31). At 23GB+ this tag alone leaves very little
# room: it will NOT co-reside with an llm-jury council (~21GB), and Ollama caps
# residency by model COUNT rather than bytes, so nothing upstream refuses that
# combination. The `qwen` wrapper shortens keep_alive to 10m on this profile for
# exactly that reason.
#
# Why 32K and not the model's native window: KV is charged num_ctx x
# OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL, so the window is the expensive knob. Bare mode keeps the
# prompt small enough that 32K is generous, and FAILOVER_LADDER hands anything
# larger to the 4B 256K tier.
#
# VERIFY THE WINDOW WITH `ollama ps`, NOT `ollama show --parameters`. An earlier
# revision of this comment had that backwards — it claimed the CONTEXT column
# always prints the model's native window and that only `--parameters` confirms
# num_ctx applied. Both halves are wrong, and believing them is what let the
# 262144 regression above sit unnoticed:
#   `ollama show --parameters` reads the TAG's stored parameter. It answers
#     "was num_ctx written to the tag", which stayed 32768 the whole time the
#     model was actually running at 262144. It cannot see the load at all.
#   `ollama ps` CONTEXT reports what the running instance ACTUALLY loaded —
#     proven above by phi4-mini printing 32768 against a 131072 native window.
# So: --parameters checks the build, ps checks the truth. Only ps can catch this.
FROM qwen3.8:27b
PARAMETER num_ctx 32768
